timestamp=2026-11-09T11:59:22.557Z level=INFO run=1ef60999 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# The value_column_name is 'yield ' (with trailing space). Also in our cleaned columns, we stripped - so cleaned column name would be 'yield'. And in the data for FARM_H, we have 'yield' (first duplicate) showing values around -500 - and offset is -500.0. Also \"calibration_offset is defined as: (recorded value) - (true value)\". So true value = recorded value - calibration_offset. So if recorded value is -500 and offset is -500, true value = (-500) - (-500) = 0. That fits if the \"recorded\" value in the raw data is what's in that column.\n# Also the other duplicate 'yield_1' is 0.0 - which could be the \"true\" or adjusted value? Or maybe the other way. But we must apply calibration according to the rule given. So we need to take the recorded value as the value from value_column_name, and true value = recorded_value - calibration_offset. So for FARM_H: recorded is the 'yield' column (first one) = -500.0, offset = -500.0, true = 0. That matches yield_1 being 0.0 in some cases? But in the data above for FARM_H, both appear in same row: yield = -500.0, yield_1 = 0.0 - so yield_1 is exactly yield - offset (since -500 - (-500)=0). That suggests yield_1 is the already calibrated/true value. But the domain rule says \"calibration_offset is defined as: (recorded value) - (true value). Any value that is negative must be dropped, this rule is valid only after the calibration offset is applied.\" So we must apply the calibration offset ourselves - meaning we compute true_value = recorded_value - calibration_offset, then drop negatives after that. That implies we take recorded values from the raw column and do the math.\n# Also the other duplicate column (yield_1 = 0) might already be calibrated. We need to be careful which column is the raw \"recorded value\".\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
