timestamp=2026-11-09T12:00:31.364Z level=INFO run=1ef60999 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Now we need to handle the case where duplicates exist. We have the value_column_name from calibration (stripped). If the exact value column name appears twice in cleaned names? e.g. 'yield' and 'yield_1'. Also calibration for FARM_H says value_column_name is 'yield ' -> stripped is 'yield'. But in the raw data for those semicolon files, maybe the duplicate is the \"other\" version. We saw that yield_1 was 0 and yield was -500 (which matches offset). That means the \"true\" or the other is yield_1? Or the recorded value is yield and true is yield_1? No wait - offset = recorded - true, so true = recorded - offset. If recorded is -500 and offset is -500, true is 0. So yield_1 being 0 matches \"true value\" after applying offset. That means in that specific file format, the second occurrence might already be calibrated? But the domain rules explicitly tell us to apply calibration_offset ourselves: \"The calibration_offset is defined as: (recorded value) - (true value).\"\n# And later: \"Any value that is negative must be dropped, this rule is valid only after the calibration offset is applied.\"\n# This means we must take recorded values, apply offset to get true values, then drop negatives. So we need to identify which column is the raw recorded value as intended. Maybe the column matching value_column_name (stripped) is the recorded value - even if there are duplicates, the one that is the \"base\" name is the raw one? Or maybe we should pick the one that is NOT the \"calibrated-looking\" one? Or look at which one has values closer to offset-scale issues?\n# For FARM_H, the base 'yield' has values around -500 (equal to offset in many cases), which suggests it's the recorded/raw reading before correction. The 'yield_1' has values 0.0 - likely after correction. So we should use the base column 'yield' (not the deduped variant) as the recorded value.\n# But how to know? Also for other farms with duplicates? Let us look at FARM_AA case - we saw reading and reading_1: reading had -50, reading_1 had 0. What is calibration for farms with these? Let us look up calibration for all farms - do any of them have value_column_name that matches those with duplicates?\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
