timestamp=2026-11-09T12:01:11.722Z level=INFO run=1ef60999 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Same pattern: base 'reading' has values equal to offset (FARM_J offset is 20000.0), reading_1 is 0. So the base column (no _N suffix) is the recorded/raw value, and the deduped variant _1 is the \"true\"/calibrated value. That means we should always use the base column name as the recorded value - i.e. the value_column_name (stripped) as it appears as the base name. So when looking for the value column, find the column whose name equals the stripped value_column_name, or if it doesn't exist due to stripping issues, find the one that starts with it and has no numeric suffix after underscore? Or just look for exact match first, then fallback to the base name (remove any _\\d+ suffix).\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
