timestamp=2026-11-09T12:14:33.643Z level=INFO run=1ef60999 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# 700 kW installed capacity. That cannot produce ~891k kWh in a day. That’s 1274 full-load hours in a day (impossible). Something is fundamentally wrong with how we’re reading the values or the units.\n# Let us look at other farms with more reasonable results. For example FARM_A: we got 3936 kWh. 800 kW capacity * ~4.9 capacity factor = plausible for solar. FARM_B: 10880 kWh, 1500 kW capacity - ~7.25 capacity factor. Also plausible depending on conditions. FARM_H: 6191 kWh, 950 kW capacity - ~6.5. These seem plausible. FARM_F’s values are just scaled up by factor ~100?\n# Compare raw numeric magnitudes before conversion across farms. For FARM_F, interval energy values are like 11, 22 kWh (per interval) - that means ~22 kWh generated in 30 min interval with 700 kW capacity? That’s average power ~44 kW during that interval - plausible. So the interval energy values themselves are plausible. But summing all intervals gives ~891k total. That would only make sense if there are ~40k intervals in the day? But we had ~69 intervals after filtering. That’s not matching. Wait how many intervals total in the reporting day for FARM_F?\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
