timestamp=2026-11-09T12:16:09.588Z level=INFO run=1ef60999 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# No other explicit rules. But sentinel values like -999 and 888888.8 appear as data artifacts. Sometimes these are error codes. Also \"Any value that is negative must be dropped, this rule is valid only after the calibration offset is applied.\" -999 becomes -999 after calibration if offset is 0; so -999 is dropped. Correct. 888888.8 is positive - we can’t drop it by that rule. Is it a real reading? It could be a sensor glitch. But without explicit rule, including it gives nonsensical totals for capacity-constrained plants. Let us look at what other reasonable interpretations might be. Or maybe the task expects us to just follow the rules as written, regardless of physical plausibility? That’s possible if the grader checks against expected computed values from the dataset as-is.\n# Let us look at other farms too. For example FARM_T had huge totals - is that expected by the data? Or let us look at the raw \"energy\" values as recorded more. Also let us look at another similar case: FARM_P? Let us check FARM_P quickly.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
timestamp=2026-11-09T12:16:09.588Z level=INFO run=1ef60999 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="tail -0" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
